---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Places, titles, and currency"
  description="The named locations in the town, the royal court terminology, and how we handle the currency unit."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Translation Canon', href: `${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/` },
    { label: 'Places and currency' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>Town and school locations</h2>

  <p>
    Most of the named places appear in both Game 1 and Game 3, so we
    use Game 1's English names. A few are new and got fresh
    translations.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Japanese</th><th>English</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>かめや</td><td>The Emporium</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ハロゲンデパート</td><td>Harrows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>フラワーデイジー</td><td>Daisy's Flowers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>スマイル・ビビ</td><td>Vivian's Boutique</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ゆうれいやしき</td><td>Haunted House</td></tr>
      <tr><td>こうどう</td><td>Auditorium</td></tr>
      <tr><td>おんしつ</td><td>Conservatory</td></tr>
      <tr><td>しょうてんがい</td><td>Shopping Avenue</td></tr>
      <tr><td>こいびとみさき</td><td>Lovers' Point</td></tr>
      <tr><td>かいがん</td><td>Beach</td></tr>
      <tr><td>かわ</td><td>River</td></tr>
      <tr><td>いけ</td><td>Pond</td></tr>
      <tr><td>もり</td><td>Woods</td></tr>
      <tr><td>とう</td><td>Tower</td></tr>
      <tr><td>おしろ</td><td>castle</td></tr>
      <tr><td>まち</td><td>Town</td></tr>
      <tr><td>りょう</td><td>Dorm</td></tr>
      <tr><td>へや</td><td>Dorm Room</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Currency: Ritch, not Mira</h2>
  <p>
    Game 1's English release used <strong>Mira</strong> as its currency
    name. The Japanese in Tongari uses <strong>リッチ (Ritchi)</strong>.
    These are different in-universe currencies — Mira was a Game 1
    convention, and Tongari's deliberately uses something else.
  </p>

  <p>
    We've settled on <strong>Ritch</strong> as the English form of
    リッチ: short, distinct, lets the Japanese pun ("rich") read
    through into English. The currency tier strings in the ARM9 menu
    pool use this spelling — "1 Ritch," "10 Ritch," up to "100,000
    Ritch."
  </p>

  <p>
    See <a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/arm9-menu-pool/`}>the
    ARM9 menu pool</a> for the byte budgets involved in fitting these
    strings into their hardcoded slots.
  </p>

  <h2>Royal court</h2>
  <p>
    The game has a king and his court that occasionally show up in
    cutscenes and special events. The titles:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>His Majesty</strong> — referring to the king. The king
      is male in this game.</li>
    <li><strong>The Court</strong> — when referring to royal officials
      collectively.</li>
    <li><strong>Minister</strong> — for だいじん, the king's chief
      advisor.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Class names</h2>
  <p>
    The school's classes get English names based on their Japanese
    subject area. Some are obvious (cooking class, magic class);
    others needed a choice:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>まほうご (Magic Words) class → <strong>Linguistics</strong>.
      This is the class where students learn the magic-language
      keyboard.</li>
    <li>まほう (Magic) class → <strong>Magic</strong>. Casting practice.</li>
    <li>がっき (Instrument) class → <strong>Music</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Retake button</h2>
  <p>
    The photo and design minigames have a "リテイク" button. Game 1
    doesn't have these, but Tyler picked <strong>Retake</strong> as the
    canonical English form — straight katakana → English with no
    surprises.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
